#+TITLE: The Indue Spellbook
#+SUBTITLE: The Spell Catalogue, by Category
#+AUTHOR: Ike Ah-Loe
#+STATUS: Pre-specification, exploratory design

* About This Book

This is the catalogue of Indue's spells, organised by category. It is a companion to *Indue, Language Specification*, which defines the syntax and the semantic model this book assumes throughout: how a spell takes inputs, where its effects land, how chains fold over property state, and how unrecognised spells recover forward.

Read the language specification to learn *how spells work*. Read this book to find out *which spells exist*.

Adding a spell to this catalogue is not a breaking change. Unknown spells degrade to their targets by design, so an implementation built against an earlier edition stays correct when it meets a spell it does not know.

** How to read an entry

Spell names follow CSS wherever a CSS property means the same thing, so that knowledge transfers. Where a CSS name is long and the spell is written often, a short alias is listed beside it and either may be used.

Two conventions are worth knowing before the tables make sense:

- *Nature.* Every spell paints either a *span* (a run of text) or a *block* (a container). Nature belongs to the spell, never to the target. Where a property is meaningful at both tiers, there are two spells: the bare name is block-natural, and a ~span-~ prefixed name is span-natural. See Span and Block in the language specification.
- *Inputs are vessels.* ~:bg<red>~, not ~:bg(red)~. A scroll ~[...]~ is used where the value is opaque text, such as a font name or a URL.

